Thanks for the quick reply and attempting to understand my data.
Here is a more detailed explanation of my design.

On point 1.   I put 72 traps out for small mammals over 6 continuous trap nights at 4 road sites and 3 continuous forest (controls).
                   So I have a total of 432 data points for each site. At each site, the traps were placed in a line transect from (0m.....210m) at a fixed 30m increment.
                   To differentiate, the location of each of my trap I have a trap number. The trap number is more for my reference cause the distance from the road will provide the same information.
                   On my control sites, the traps were placed at the same distance but because there is no road, the distance is meaningless.

On my data: Y1=presence/absence of small mammal in trap
                   Trap night = Day of trapping (between 1-6)
                   Site = Site 1 to 7
                   Treatment = Road or a control site
                   Road width = Width of the road
                   Species = species of small mammal when caught in trap
                   Age = age of the small mammal
                   Sex = gender of the small mammal

My aim is to fit my data to model small mammal occupancy of road side habitats versus continuous forest sites while accounting for imperfect detectability.

Just to make sure my data is in the right format, under the following
Detection history should have : Site, Trap Night??, Y1 . Since the output of Y1(the presence/ absence is dependent on Site x number of trap night at each site)
Site specific covariates: Treatment, Road Width, Date sampled
Observation covariates: Species, Age and Sex

Am I on the right track?

